#!/usr/bin/python3
"""i3 hotkeys on the Linux text console.

On a text VT nothing listens for the i3 chords. This daemon reads every
keyboard's evdev node and, when a chord is pressed while the foreground VT
is in text mode (KD_TEXT, i.e. not X), acts for the user who owns the VT:

  Win+1 .. Win+9, Win+0  select tmux window 1 .. 9, 10 in the tmux session
                         attached to that VT, like i3's workspace keys;
  Win+Shift+S            toggle suspend-on-lid-close: runs the same
                         ~/.config/i3/toggle-lid-suspend.sh as i3, so X and
                         the console share one state (the script's
                         systemd-inhibit process and pidfile);
  volume up/down, mute   the commands of the i3 config (pamixer), run as the
                         VT's owner in their PipeWire session;
  brightness up/down     brightnessctl, as in the i3 config (never to 0);
  Win held               the i3 bar (i3's "mode hide" bar, "modifier Mod4");
  Win+Escape             lock all consoles, like i3's xsecurelock: vlock -a
                         on a spare VT, as the VT's owner, asking for that
                         user's password (see Lock).

It also types text into the console for its owner on request, through a
socket (see TypeService): nerd-dictation's dictated text.

The bar is the bottom row of the VT, drawn into the screen memory
(/dev/vcsaN) over whatever program is there: the user's own i3blocks runs
persistently as that user and its i3bar JSON stream is the right part.
Feedback (lid state, volume, brightness, errors) pops up in the same bar,
at the left, for a few seconds - nothing is written into the tty, which
would corrupt full-screen programs. When the bar goes away, the row is put
back wherever the screen still shows the bar; what the program wrote
meanwhile is kept.

Keys that the console keymap toggle file (KEYMAP_TOGGLE in
/etc/vconsole.conf) maps on purpose are left to the console: on a laptop
whose Fn+Left/Right arrive as volume keys and are mapped to Home/End there,
they stay Home/End.

The kernel console has no Super modifier, so Win+1 would also type "1" into
the program. The daemon therefore turns both Win keys into the kernel's
ShiftL modifier in the console keymap: the keymap has no tables for ShiftL
combinations, so the kernel emits nothing for Win+key - except cursor and
CSI keys (arrows), which it sends with the modifier encoded. The original
keymap entries are restored on exit.
"""
